    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `Client._init_proxy_transport`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_init_proxy_transport` function in the `httpx._client` module is responsible for initializing and returning an HTTPTransport object configured to use a specified proxy. This function is essential for setting up a transport layer that can route HTTP requests through a proxy server, which is useful for scenarios like network restrictions, anonymization, or accessing resources behind a firewall.\n\nThe function accepts several parameters to customize the transport behavior:\n- `proxy`: An instance of the `Proxy` class that defines the proxy server details.\n- `ssl_context`: An optional SSL context for secure connections. If not provided, it defaults to `None`.\n- `http1` and `http2`: Boolean flags to enable or disable HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 protocols, respectively. By default, HTTP/1.1 is enabled, and HTTP/2 is disabled.\n- `limits`: An instance of the `Limits` class that sets various limits on the transport, such as the maximum number of connections. It defaults to `DEFAULT_LIMITS`.\n- `trust_env`: A boolean indicating whether to trust environment variables for configuration. It defaults to `True`.\n- `verify` and `cert`: Deprecated parameters that were used for SSL verification and certificate configuration. They are now superseded by the `ssl_context` parameter.\n\nThe function returns an `HTTPTransport` object configured with the provided parameters. It relies on the `HTTPTransport` class constructor to create and configure the transport object. The function does not handle any exceptions or edge cases explicitly; instead, it relies on the `HTTPTransport` class to handle any issues that arise during initialization.\n\nThere are no side effects in this function. It does not modify any global state or external resources. It simply creates and returns a new `HTTPTransport` object based on the input parameters.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into at